# Carrier Wound #38: The Planner Self-Reported Why It Couldn't Plan, And The Carrier Redacted The Reason To A Bare Key Name. A Correct Block You Cannot Read Is A Coin Toss.

Wound #37 (both halves) un-blinded the *verifier* and re-derived its size
ceiling. The next live dream drives never reached the verifier. They went
terminal one gate earlier, in the **Workflow planning step**, before a single
verifier check ran:

```
run blocked: planner_output_invalid
  "Planner lane returned output that failed the blueprint contract
   (planner-output, deterministic): present top-level keys [error],
   missing required keys [harness, machine, plan, verificationContract],
   schema issue paths [...]"
```

The `sandbox.exec` SUCCEEDED — `exitCode: 0`, `durationMs ≈ 166_812` (~166s of
real planner work), valid JSON, `outputNormalization.normalized === true`. This
was **not** the no-parseable-output branch (wound #33/#34), **not** an incomplete
agent-lane (`normalized === false`, wound #28). The planner pi-agent ran to
completion and emitted a well-formed envelope:

```json
{ "error": "<the actual reason it could not produce a blueprint>" }
```

The carrier did the **right** thing: pi emitted an *error self-report*, not a
blueprint, so `PlannerLaneBlueprintDocumentSchema.safeParse` failed and the run
blocked `planner_output_invalid`. That block is correct and stays — a planner
that says "I can't" must not be waved through as if it produced a plan.

## The wound: the block was correct and completely unreadable

`PlannerBlueprintContractError` is built to be redaction-safe by design (wound
#27): it surfaces only top-level **key names**, never **values**, because a
planner output could in principle contain a path, a secret, a customer payload.
So the operator-facing block said `present top-level keys [error]` — it named the
field and **redacted the one value that explains the failure.** The planner told
us *exactly* why it couldn't plan, in plain workflow-design prose, and the
carrier threw that prose on the floor and handed back the word "error."

The reason was not gone — it was pinned in a content-addressed `ARTIFACTS`
artifact. But reading it meant reverse-engineering the Workers Artifacts store
format and re-running a 166s exec just to dig a tail that still couldn't reach
the string. A live block whose only diagnosis path is "spend three minutes
mining a content-addressed blob" is, operationally, a block you **guess** at. And
the governing demand for this whole effort is the opposite of guessing:

> *"feels like we should be able to observe the run in progress and not guess on
> if its functioning instead of waiting and staring at it like fucking dummies."*

The class is **diagnosability collapse at a correct gate**: the safety
property (don't leak values) was applied so bluntly that it suppressed the *one*
value that is itself the diagnosis. Observability's whole promise is the inverse:

> "By gathering application telemetry, at the right level of abstraction,
> aggregated around the user's experience, with the ability to analyze it in real
> time, we gained magical insights. We removed the limitations of our traditional
> tools once we had the capability to ask any question, trace any sequence of
> steps, and understand any internal system state, simply by observing the
> outputs of our applications."
> — Charity Majors, Liz Fong-Jones, George Miranda, *Observability Engineering*,
>   Ch. 3

We could not "understand the internal system state simply by observing the
outputs" — because the carrier had redacted the output that carried the state.

## The fix: narrow the "never values" rule by exactly one diagnostic exception

This is the wound #34 precedent (`rawOutputSample` → blocker) applied to the
planner-output path, which had never received it. A new redaction-safe sampler,
`redactionSafePlannerErrorEnvelopeSample` (`src/app/application/ports.ts`),
crosses **only** the `error` field of a **recognized `{error: ...}` envelope**,
bounded at 512 chars, whitespace collapsed to a single line, with a
`…[sample truncated]` marker on overflow. `PlannerBlueprintContractError` gains an
`errorEnvelopeSample` field and appends it to its message:
`…, planner error envelope: <reason>`. The factory
(`plannerBlueprintContractError`, `cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts`) computes
the sample from the raw output it already holds at the throw site.

### Why narrowing "never values" is safe *here* and nowhere else

The "never values" rule (wound #27) is correct as a default and is **not**
relaxed in general. The exception is scoped to a single, provable condition: the
value crosses **only** when the output is a recognized error envelope, and
**only** the `error` field crosses. Justification, receipt-grounded:

- The planner lane runs `--no-tools` (wound #31, gated at the CLI to
  `LANE_KIND=planner`), so it cannot materialize a secret or read a private path
  at all.
- Its prompt forbids secret materialization. A pi-emitted `error` is therefore
  **workflow-design reasoning** — "no pinned package exports a skill providing
  capability X; cannot satisfy verificationContract" — never a credential, a
  customer payload, or a private filesystem path.
- A non-error output (a `{result}` envelope, a partial blueprint, a scalar)
  returns `null` from the sampler, so its values **never** cross. Only the
  diagnostic self-report does.

The block **still fires.** Visibility never silences the check — the run is still
`planner_output_invalid`, still terminal, still refusing to treat "I can't" as a
plan. The only thing that changed is the operator can now *read why* on the
status surface instead of mining an artifact.

## What this fix does NOT decide (the layer-2 fork stays open)

This makes the reason **visible**; it does not classify it. Once the next live
drive prints `planner error envelope: <reason>`, the reason decides the next
move, and the two branches are genuinely different wounds:

- **Transient** (model hiccup, a flaky package-metadata read, a momentary
  capability-discovery miss) → re-drive; the planner likely succeeds on retry and
  the run proceeds past this gate. No further carrier change.
- **Structural** (the planner *correctly* concludes the pinned package set cannot
  satisfy the requested capability for this dream shape) → that is a **true signal
  about the cartridge/package pinning**, a distinct wound to fix at the
  cartridge/discovery layer — **not** to be silenced by loosening the blueprint
  contract or by pretending an error envelope is a plan.

Re-blinding the block to make the run go green would be the planner-side twin of
removing the verifier check: it ships the harm (a run that proceeds on a planner
that said "I can't"). Forbidden.

## The chaos tests that would have caught it (hostile transport, not polite mocks)

Two layers in `tests/integration/cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.test.ts`, both
falsified (red on the bug, green on the fix):

- **Layer 1 — end-to-end through the real adapter.** A new case drives the
  **real** `createCloudflarePiPlannerLaneAdapter`, injects a planner output of
  `{ error: "<a real no-capability reason> ; cannot satisfy verificationContract" }`
  as pi's *own self-report transport* (a hostile double of pi emitting an error
  envelope, **not** a hand-crafted `ZodError`), and asserts the rejection is a
  `PlannerBlueprintContractError` whose **message carries the full reason**
  (`messageCarriesReason: true`), whose `presentKeys` is `["error"]`, and that the
  block is still `deterministic: true`. **Falsified:** drop the
  `errorEnvelopeSample` wiring and `messageCarriesReason` goes false — the reason
  vanishes from the message exactly as it did live.
- **Layer 2 — the sampler's bounds, directly.** A unit case pins the
  redaction-safety boundary: a recognized `{error}` is sampled with whitespace
  collapsed; a non-error `{result}` envelope, a scalar string, and a
  whitespace-only error all return `null` (non-error values **never** cross); a
  non-string error object is `JSON.stringify`'d; and a 900-char error truncates to
  exactly `512 + "…[sample truncated]".length` with the marker. This is the
  guard that keeps the "one diagnostic exception" from widening into a values
  leak.

The pre-existing `{result}`-shape contract test stays **green** untouched —
`{result}` has no `error` field, so the sampler returns `null`, no message suffix
appears, and the block reads `present top-level keys [result]` exactly as before.
The exception is additive and inert on every non-error output.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · full suite **442 passed / 55 files**.

## The carrier discipline (eighteenth carrier wound)

#35 fixed *whether the verifier could speak.* #36 fixed *a check that refused
every answer.* #37 fixed *what the verifier sees before it answers* and *how big
that can be.* #38 steps back one gate earlier, to the planner, and fixes a
different failure of the same family: **a correct block is worth nothing if the
operator cannot read why it fired.** The "never values" redaction rule was right
as a default and wrong as an absolute — it suppressed the single value that is
the diagnosis. The class is not "the planner emitted an error." It is "**a
safety-motivated redaction was applied so bluntly it hid the self-report that
explains the failure, turning a correct, deterministic block into a guess.**" The
fix narrows the rule by exactly one provable exception — a bounded sample of a
recognized error envelope from a `--no-tools`, no-secrets lane — and proves with
a hostile double that the block still fires and now *reads*. A block you cannot
read is, operationally, a coin toss; and a coin toss at a safety gate is the
thing this entire burn-in exists to kill. Linked: [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]].

## Honest live outcome: the block did NOT recur — the fork resolved TRANSIENT

The carrier discipline demands the receipt say what actually happened on the
deployed code, not what the wound *predicted*. So: **the wound #38 block did not
fire on the live re-drive.** Run `run-live-20260615T012808096Z-83eee46c`,
submitted ~18:28 and observed live by polling `/runs/:runId/status`, ran the
planner for ~6 minutes (`planningDynamicWorkflow` from 18:28:51 through 18:34:51)
and then produced a **valid blueprint** — it pinned the plan artifact, resolved
the capsule, pinned packages, loaded the pinned dynamic workflow, executed,
verified for ~2 minutes, delivered the review surface, and reached **`captured`
(terminal=true, blocker=none) at 18:44:54**. No `planner_output_invalid` block,
no `PlannerBlueprintContractError`, no error envelope to sample.

That means the prior drive's `{error}` envelope was **transient** — exactly the
left branch of the layer-2 fork above (a model/discovery hiccup, not a structural
"this cartridge cannot satisfy the contract" verdict). The honest reading: the
fix shipped as **validated latent observability insurance**, not as the thing
that unblocked this run. It changed nothing about *this* drive's path; it changed
what the *next* planner self-report will be able to tell us. If a future drive
prints `planner error envelope: <reason>`, the operator now reads the reason
instead of a bare `[error]`, and the fork gets decided on evidence. Shipping a
correct, falsified, hostile-double-tested fix whose live trigger never fired this
round is not a wasted fix — it is the gauge installed before the next time the
needle moves. It is also the governing goal met: **a dream-shaped run actually
reached `captured` on the deployed code, observed live.** Discord ping fired
(operator-side DM, not a workflow side effect) with the review URL.
